  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss. It is 4 times steeper in x, so overshoot starts above learning rate 0.25 and swings grow (diverge) above 0.5.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per axis is Gaussian with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63b)) for batch size b: 2 at b=1, exactly 0 at b=64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows show steps (learning rate × slope), not the slope itself, drawn at 3× their true length in bowl units. The orange one is the move the ball makes next; the grey one is the step full data would give.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when loss passes 8 or the ball leaves the drawn square. Off-bowl positions are drawn at the edge; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence. 'New run, keep last path' uses the next noise values and keeps the finished path as the ghost.",
    "While running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at speed 1. Changing a setting mid-run affects the next steps."
  ]
}